CPU vs GPU vs TPU vs NPU (Comparison)

Introduction:
👉🏻 Modern computing uses different types of processors depending on the task:

•CPU: General purpose computing
•GPU: Parallel processing (graphics + AI)
•TPU: Google’s accelerator for neural networks
•NPU: AI-focused processing unit found in phones/ laptops


1) CPU (Central Processing Unit)

🔹 Key Role
•Handles general-purpose tasks
•Runs operating systems and most software

🔹 Strengths
•Great at serial processing (one step after another)
•Strong control logic (decision-making, system tasks)
•Flexible for many types of workloads

🔹 Weaknesses
•Not optimized for heavy parallel operations (like matrix multiplication)
•Slower for large-scale AI training/inference compared to GPUs/TPUs

🧾 Example Uses
•Web browsing, operating system tasks
•Running databases, spreadsheets
•General application processing


 2) GPU (Graphics Processing Unit)

🔹 Key Role
•Designed for massively parallel computations
•Widely used for deep learning

🔹 Strengths
•Thousands of cores → performs many operations simultaneously
•Very efficient for matrix multiplication and neural network computations
•Works well for both training and inference

🔹 Weaknesses
•Power hungry (compared to NPUs for mobile)
•Programming can be more complex than CPU

🧾 Example Uses
•Video rendering
•Gaming
•AI training/inference (e.g., using CUDA)


3) TPU (Tensor Processing Unit)

🔹 Key Role
•Specialized AI chip optimized for neural networks
•Developed by Google

🔹 Strengths
•Highly optimized for tensor operations
•Faster and more power-efficient for AI workloads than general chips
•Often used in large-scale cloud AI systems

🔹 Weaknesses
•Less general-purpose (not for every task)
•Mostly used in Google ecosystems/cloud setups

🧾 Example Uses
•Google cloud AI services
•Large deep learning model training/inference


📱 4) NPU (Neural Processing Unit)

🔹 Key Role
•Dedicated to AI inference (and sometimes limited training)
•Common in mobile devices and edge devices

🔹 Strengths
•Very energy efficient
•Low latency inference (fast responses)
•Helps run AI features directly on the device (no cloud needed)

🔹 Weaknesses
•Limited flexibility compared to CPU/GPU
•Performance depends on device and model optimization

🧾 Example Uses
•Face recognition
•Speech recognition
•Camera enhancements and real-time AI


Conclusion:

•CPU is best for general tasks and system control.
•GPU is ideal when you need heavy parallel processing, especially in AI.
•TPU is a specialized chip for fast neural network (tensor) processing at scale.
•NPU is built for efficient AI on devices, enabling fast and power-saving inference.